{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011WJ1bAffb1fg7swR5DoFbD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Release and maintenance evidence\n2\t\n3\tThe planning, design, build, test, deployment, and maintenance stages use the same\n4\tatomic lifecycle. Release and monitoring remain in existing external systems.\n5\tA release-readiness task should bind exact source revision and artifact digest,\n6\ttarget environment, CI/CD run, required checks, rollback evidence, and the human\n7\trelease decision. Its evals verify those claims at the appropriate target.\n8\t\n9\t```bash\n10\ttaskspec example release-evidence --out release-input.json\n11\ttaskspec receipt operational validate release-input.json\n12\ttaskspec receipt operational import release-input.json --out .taskspec/operations/release.json\n13\t```\n14\t\n15\tOperational imports retain source digests and reported provenance. `--verify-local`\n16\tchecks attachment bytes against declared digests; it does not authenticate the\n17\texternal reporter or verify target health. Import never grants acceptance.\n18\tUse a separate authorized task to independently verify the target when required.\n19\t\n20\tFor incidents, use `taskspec example incident --out incident.json`, record the\n21\tactual observation, affected revision, environment, impact, and source, then import\n22\tit with the same receipt command. `decompose init <id> --intent-file incident.json`\n23\tcreates candidate intent. Diagnose with a bounded diagnose-repair-verify recipe.\n24\tRegression additions, policies, skills, and project knowledge changes require\n25\treviewed scoped tasks; observation alone never authorizes them.\n26\t\n27\tThe lifecycle mapping is informed by [Anthropic's AI-native SDLC playbook](https://claude.com/blog/the-ai-native-sdlc-playbook).\n28\tTaskSpec's contracts and acceptance boundaries are implementation choices.\n29\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9aa51949-e1ca-4b77-8ee3-efab86480a5c","uuid":"cc53f948-4b28-4d22-a2e2-b4f0b80fea27","timestamp":"2026-09-15T11:18:41.618Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/zf/r0gsd5px2v9_q24j3tfnyb5c0000gn/T/taskspec chat 50lvl10m/.agents/skills/task-spec/docs/guides/toolkit/sdlc.md","content":"# Release and maintenance evidence\n\nThe planning, design, build, test, deployment, and maintenance stages use the same\natomic lifecycle. Release and monitoring remain in existing external systems.\nA release-readiness task should bind exact source revision and artifact digest,\ntarget environment, CI/CD run, required checks, rollback evidence, and the human\nrelease decision. Its evals verify those claims at the appropriate target.\n\n```bash\ntaskspec example release-evidence --out release-input.json\ntaskspec receipt operational validate release-input.json\ntaskspec receipt operational import release-input.json --out .taskspec/operations/release.json\n```\n\nOperational imports retain source digests and reported provenance. `--verify-local`\nchecks attachment bytes against declared digests; it does not authenticate the\nexternal reporter or verify target health. Import never grants acceptance.\nUse a separate authorized task to independently verify the target when required.\n\nFor incidents, use `taskspec example incident --out incident.json`, record the\nactual observation, affected revision, environment, impact, and source, then import\nit with the same receipt command. `decompose init <id> --intent-file incident.json`\ncreates candidate intent. Diagnose with a bounded diagnose-repair-verify recipe.\nRegression additions, policies, skills, and project knowledge changes require\nreviewed scoped tasks; observation alone never authorizes them.\n\nThe lifecycle mapping is informed by [Anthropic's AI-native SDLC playbook](https://claude.com/blog/the-ai-native-sdlc-playbook).\nTaskSpec's contracts and acceptance boundaries are implementation choices.\n","numLines":29,"startLine":1,"totalLines":29}}}
